>> A2: Briefly describe how you implemented argument parsing.  How do
>> you arrange for the elements of argv[] to be in the right order?
>> How do you avoid overflowing the stack page?

We avoided overflow by using the realloc function when loading argv from the buffer. In load(), the arguments were loaded in the order they 
appear, and in setup_stack the arguments are looped through backwards and placed into the stack pointer (esp). The stack is then loaded with 
the addresses of the argvarray, followed with the address of argv itself, and finally a "return address" (which is set to 0 according to the 
project specification.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A3: Why does Pintos implement strtok_r() but not strtok()?

strtok_r() is a thread-safe and reentrant function, whereas strtok() is neither. strtok_r() uses a save pointer instead of a static variable 
to keep track of the string we're tokenizing, that way multiple threads can safely access the same string without risking race conditions.

>> A4: In Pintos, the kernel separates commands into a executable name
>> and arguments.  In Unix-like systems, the shell does this
>> separation.  Identify at least two advantages of the Unix approach.

1. By delegating to the shell, the kernel can focus on more pertinent tasks like process scheduling.
2. It also simplifies security. When a shell executes a command, the executable inherits the privileges of the shell process. 
   Pushing this responsibility to the shell avoids improper privilege escalation by a malicious program.
